title:: reCording
summary:: Recorders, replays and takes as objects held by a song; scores of what you did
categories:: Libraries>reCording
related:: Classes/RERecorder, Classes/REReplay, Classes/RETake, Classes/REScore, Classes/REScoreRecorder, Classes/RETap, Classes/REJSON, Guides/reCurrent

section:: What it is

The pieces recorded through global functions (code::~start_recording::, code::~stop_recording::, code::~start_playback_file::) redefined by every recorder block. reCording replaces them with objects a song holds by name: link::Classes/RERecorder:: (a set of tracks recorded together under a piece folder), link::Classes/REReplay:: (a take cued into a proxy or a bus) and link::Classes/RETake:: (paths, and the record-a-replay session used for AmbiX conversions). What is recorded varies per scene, so a song keeps a dictionary of recorders and of replays (link::Classes/RCSong#-addRecorder::, link::Classes/RCSong#-addReplay::); registering under an existing name frees the previous object, and code::clearAll:: frees them all.

Next to the sound, a song can record strong::what you did:: to make it: knob and slider gestures, OSC messages, MIDI notes, actions on the reCurrent objects and the code evaluated in the IDE, as a link::Classes/REScore:: (a score, saved as JSON) that replays live, becomes patterns, or renders offline. See link::#Scores: recording what you do::.

section:: Usage

code::
(
~song.addRecorder(\ambix, RERecorder(s, ~piece_dir +/+ "RecorderSC", "LeafHues/AmbiX", "w1", numChannels: 16)
	.addTrack('AmbiX Out', ~song.ambi.decoder)).prepare;
)
~song.recorder(\ambix).start([4, 0]);            // on the song's clock
~song.recorder(\ambix).stop;

// replay an earlier take into the chain and record the conversion
(
~song.addReplay(\take, REReplay(s, ~original_root +/+ "LeafHues/take.aiff", 16, ampDb: 0)
	.into(~song.ambi.signal)).prepare;
)
RETake.session(~song.recorder(\ambix), ~song.replay(\take), minutes: 9, seconds: 24, quant: [4, 0]);
::

section:: Timing

A recorder starts at a quantized beat of its clock (the song's once registered). link::Classes/Recorder#-record:: sends immediately while the replay's DiskIn synth is a latency-stamped bundle, so a conversion take starts code::server.latency:: early unless code::start(alignToLatency: true):: is used. code::stopAfter:: counts seconds on SystemClock, whatever the tempo.

section:: Scores: recording what you do

A score is the performance without the sound: every gesture, message, note, action and code line, with the beat it happened on. One link::Classes/REScoreRecorder:: per song, made on first use:

code::
~rec = ~song.scoreRecorder(root: ~piece_dir +/+ "Scores", version: "w1");
~rec.arm;                                        // the whole song, every input
~rec.record([4, 0]);                             // the take starts on the next bar
~rec.snapshot(\intro);                           // the state of everything in scope, by name
~rec.stop;                                       // -> an REScore, written to <root>/<stamp>_<song> w1.json
~rec.disarm;                                     // off: nothing installed, nothing checked
::

subsection:: Off, armed, recording

Recording is off by default and off means off. The reCurrent methods that can be recorded hold one line, code::if(RETap.active) { RETap.action(...) }::, and when nothing is armed that is one Boolean check: no wrapper, no indirection, nothing scheduled, nothing installed. The IDE hooks (link::#Code::) and the recorder itself exist only between code::arm:: and code::disarm::. Armed or recording, the recorder only observes: it appends to a list, never draws a random number, never touches the clock queue or the server, and writes the file at code::stop::. Every tap runs under link::Classes/RCGuard::, so an error in the recorder is logged and the action proceeds. The suite's identity test runs the same scripted session with recording off and on and asserts that the beats' sources, the random state and the clock queue are the same, the score being the only difference.

subsection:: Scope and inputs

code::
~rec.arm(scope: [~song.layer(\core), ~batch, ~song.layer(\meta).beat(\k)], inputs: #[\midi, \actions]);
::

strong::scope:: is a set of objects (layers, beats, batches, orgnsms, fobjects, crawlers, MIDI mappings, OSC defs); nil means the whole song. An event is kept when its object, or the object's layer or batch, is in the scope. strong::inputs:: selects the kinds of event (all by default):

table::
## code::\midi:: || the mappings of link::Classes/RCMidi:: (code::control::, code::controlSynth::, code::controlAttribute::): the raw CC value and the mapped value
## code::\osc:: || the defs of link::Classes/RCOsc::: the path and the message arguments (the remote page sends OSC too). A message that came in a bundle with a timetag is stamped at that time when it lies within code::REScoreRecorder.maxLag:: seconds before now (the page stamps each gesture at the touch, so the network's delay is not in the take); a timetag of now, of 1 or of a clock that is off stamps now
## code::\keyboard:: || link::Classes/RCKeyboardState::: note on/off, bend, pressure, CC per channel
## code::\actions:: || the public methods of the reCurrent objects listed under link::#What is recorded::
## code::\code:: || the lines evaluated in the IDE
## code::\rawMidi:: || every MIDI message of every device, as sent (note on/off, control, bend, touch, polytouch, program; voice: the device's name). Not on by default: it replaces code::\midi:: and code::\keyboard::, because a raw message replays by dispatching through MIDIIn again, so the piece's own MIDIdefs, the song's mappings and its keyboard fire as they did. The choice: code::\midi:: and code::\keyboard:: give editable mapped values for a piece built on code::song.midi:: and code::song.keyboard::; code::\rawMidi:: captures anything, raw.
::

Voices can be named at arm or added later: code::~rec.addVoice(\roto, ~song.midi.names("Roto-Control")):: groups a device's mappings, code::~rec.addVoice(\lead, [~beat1, ~beat2])::, code::~rec.removeVoice(\lead)::; code::arm(voicesByLayer: true):: makes a layer the default voice of its beats; code::~rec.voiceFor(obj):: tells. code::~rec.addControl(\knob_mix, ControlSpec(0, 1)):: gives the takes the spec of a control (a view scales its curve by it, a morph ramps along its warp).

subsection:: Your actions, not the program's

OSC and MIDI handlers, IDE code, GUI actions and clock-scheduled Functions run on sclang's main thread; patterns, control loops and replays run in Routines. The tap records a call only from the main thread: what a pattern does to its orgnsms is the program's own work and the seed regenerates it. Library code that reaches a recordable method from a scheduled Function (a beat freeing itself when its pattern ends) uses a private twin that bypasses the tap. A piece that automates with clock-scheduled Functions wraps them in code::RETap.silently { }::, or lives with those events showing up (they carry no cause and the editor lists them).

Each event knows its strong::cause::. A MIDI message, an OSC message or a code line opens a cause; the actions it triggers are its effects. Effects are recorded only under a code line (its fallback at replay and its explanation in the view); under a message they are not: the message itself replays exactly, through the same mapping or handler, and a knob turning over a batch of 24 orgnsms would otherwise write 24 events per message.

subsection:: What is recorded

Actions, as the receiver, the method and its arguments:

table::
## link::Classes/RCBeat:: || code::set::, code::setAll::, code::play::, code::pause::, code::resume::, code::stop::, code::free::
## link::Classes/RCLayer:: || code::addBeat::, code::deleteBeat::, code::killAll::, code::pauseAll::, code::resumeAll::
## link::Classes/RCSong:: || code::killAllBeats::, code::pauseAllBeats::, code::resumeAllBeats::, code::clearAll::, code::seed_::
## link::Classes/RCBatch:: || code::addCreate::, code::startPrepared::, code::editAttr::, code::apply::, code::deleteBeats::, code::free::
## link::Classes/RCOrgnsm:: || code::rPut::, code::create::, code::start::, code::free::
## link::Classes/RCCrawler:: || code::init::, code::initFromBatch::, code::setVal::, code::jumpNext::, code::setNextVal::, code::createPattern::, code::free::
## link::Classes/RCFObject:: || code::create::, code::rPut::, code::setArg::, code::setArgArray::, code::setCenter::, code::setOrigin::, code::setRotationMatrix::, code::setWidth::, code::free::
## link::Classes/RCDurList:: || code::put::, code::addHit::, code::removeHit::, code::totalDur_::, code::duplicate:: (under the beat that owns the list)
## link::Classes/RCSwing:: || code::amount_::, code::mod_::, code::shift_:: (under the layer that holds the swing)
::

A subclass that specialises one of these overrides its private twin (link::Classes/RCPathControl:: overrides code::prStart::, not code::start::): the public method records, then dispatches.

Inputs: MIDI mappings (name, raw and mapped value), OSC defs (key, path, arguments), keyboard messages (channel, note, velocity, bend, pressure, CC), code lines (text). Snapshots: the sources of every key of the beats in scope, the static attributes of the orgnsms, the registered controls' values. A take also starts with a snapshot (code::record(startSnapshot: false):: skips it) so that it replays from silence.

subsection:: Voices

Every event belongs to a strong::voice::, the unit of overdubbing and of the lanes in the view: by default the object's name (code::core/kick::, the batch's name, the orgnsm's name), the mapping's or def's key, the keyboard's device, or code::code::. code::arm(voices: (lead: [~beat1, ~beat2], fx: [\knob_mix])):: groups objects and mapping names under names of your own.

subsection:: Levels

A take has two levels. strong::Level 1:: is what a human did: the inputs, the code lines, the snapshots, with the actions a code line triggered as its effects (the fallback). Replaying level 1 alone gives the performance again, the program regenerating its own work from the seed. strong::Level 2:: is the program's work: the actions of the tagged Routines, and an action on the main thread with no cause (a clock-scheduled Function, a handler of a server reply) while code lines are recorded. A strong::tagged Routine:: (code::RETap.routine { }::, code::RETap.task::, link::Classes/RERoutine::) carries the cause open when it was made (the line or the input) and the object whose work it does (code::by::); a beat's player is one (its pattern's and its events' actions are the beat's). A plain code::Routine::, code::Task::, code::fork:: or a pattern played by hand is seen by no one: the check flags the lines that start one. Level 2 is off by default (code::arm(level2: true):: turns it on): its events are counted and the check reports them. On, they go to a companion file next to the take (a doer writing faster than code::rateGuard:: events a second, when set, is sub-sampled to one event per control per code::rateQuantum:: beat), code::<take>.l2.json::, in the same id space, each with its code::cause:: (the level 1 event, or the level 2 event, it descends from) and its code::by:: (the object whose loop did it), and code::REScore.read:: loads both (code::levels: 1:: the take alone). Edits apply to the whole and code::write:: splits the levels again.

At replay a level 2 event plays only when the level 1 event it descends from was strong::deferred:: (code::player.defer([\code])::, or code::defer: true:: on the event) or failed without doing anything first: the source is not fired, its effects and its program events are, so a line's work can be edited event by event. Under a fired source the program does its own work and the recorded level 2 events stay silent; an orphan (no root) plays only with code::playOrphans::.

The strong::check:: (link::Classes/REScore#-check::, run at code::stop:: and written as the take's code::check::) lists what threatens an exact replay: lines not replayed, lines that raised, lines drawing random numbers or reading the clock, program actions not recorded, actions recorded without code lines among the inputs (a scheduled one plays twice). A take holds the main thread's random state at its start (code::randData::) and the player's Routine starts from it, so a line that draws draws what it drew, as long as the same lines and inputs run in the same order; a draw elsewhere on the main thread in between (a clock-scheduled Function, a GUI) breaks the sequence from there on.

strong::Level 3:: is the server's messages: what the song sent to scsynth while the take recorded, off by default (code::arm(level3: true)::). An link::Classes/RETapAddr:: stands in for the song's server address from the take's start to its stop, forwarding everything and keeping the messages that make sound (nodes, groups, buses, buffers, defs by path; not a def's bytes, a sync or a query) as code::server:: events at the time the bundle is due (code::latency:: is the bundle's offset), each under the cause open on the main thread (a line, an input) or the tagged Routine's, with its doer. They go to code::<take>.l3.json:: (code::levels:: says so; code::REScore.read(path, levels: 3):: loads them) and the SynthDefs they name are written next to the take at stop (code::<take>.defs/::). A replay ignores them: the program makes the sound. A render sends them as they were (HomewareSC's code::scripts/take.sh render --level 3::, the piece's files loaded first for the output chain and the buses, the take's defs loaded from its folder): the bridge to NRT without the program, and to editing single notes. code::take.sh render --record-level3 <folder>:: records a replay's messages as a level 3 take, and code::take.sh check:: renders a take both ways and asserts the same sound (a seed synth at time 0 makes scsynth's random generators the same).

subsection:: Replay (link::Classes/REScorePlayer::)

code::
~p = REScorePlayer(REScore.read(path), ~song);
~p.play([4, 0]);                                   // on the next bar
~p.play(from: 8, to: 16, loop: true);              // a span, again and again
~p.mute(['core/kick']); ~p.solo([\lead]); ~p.stop;
~p.onEvent = { |p, ev| ... };                      // the playhead feed of a view
::

An event replays at the recorded beat on the song's clock, from a single Routine that waits between events; with the server running, what an event sends goes in a code::server.latency:: bundle, in line with the pattern events; nothing the player does is recorded. Between two points of an input control (a MIDI, OSC, raw MIDI or keyboard value) the player ramps, code::stepsPerBeat:: times a beat, along the control's spec when the take carries one (most SynthDefs do not smooth their arguments; code::interpolate = false:: plays the points only). Before playing, the take's commits are compared with the checkouts: other code is reported once. A MIDI event calls its mapping's action with the recorded mapped value (the valFunc is not re-applied: the value is what you edit), an OSC event calls its def's function with the recorded arguments, an action calls the method on the object resolved by name, a keyboard message updates the song's keyboard, a code line is compiled and run in the current environment, a snapshot sets the recorded sources, a morph ramps to them. When a message's mapping or def no longer exists, an object is not found, or a code line raises, the event's recorded effects replay instead, except the ones the event did again before failing: a code line's actions are watched while it runs, and a line that raises after three of them plays its fourth effect on, never the first three twice. code::fallback: false:: on the event plays none of its effects, code::fallback: true:: all of them. What cannot replay is skipped with one warning each.

A replay starts on the take's own phase of the grid: with code::play([4, 0]):: the start is the next beat that is, modulo 4, where the take's code::beat0:: was, so a line or a Routine the take quantized to that grid lands as it did; code::play:: alone takes the grid the take was recorded on (code::record(quant):: writes it as code::quant::), else the beat. code::alignPhase = false:: restores the plain grid.

A score's continuous controls (link::Classes/REScore#-controlKeys::) become patterns, code::score.curvePattern([\midi, \knob_mix])::, a Pseg between the points held at both ends; its notes a Pbind per voice, code::score.notePattern('Seaboard RISE 2')::. Rendering a take offline belongs to the rig that knows which synth each control reaches (HomewareSC's remote rig).

subsection:: Overdub

code::
~rec.overdub(score, voices: [\lead], mode: \replace, punch: [8, 16], quant: [4, 0]);
~rec.overdub(score, punch: [8, 16], loop: true);   // every voice, auto mode, each pass an overdub
~rec.stop;                                         // -> the merged score, written as a new file
::

The old take plays (link::Classes/REScorePlayer::) while a new one is recorded on the same timeline; code::stop:: merges them (link::Classes/REScore#*merge::) and writes the merge as a new file, the original staying. Modes, per voice: strong::replace:: mutes the voice's old events from the punch-in and drops them within the punch; strong::keep:: adds the new events to the old; strong::touch:: drops, per control, the old events between the first and last touch of it; strong::nil:: (the default) is touch for continuous controls and replace for the voice's other events (notes, transport, messages): two curves on one slider would fight, two notes do not. In a loop the passes fold into the span and, unless strong::keep::, the last pass of each voice (each control, for touch) remains. New events of voices not listed are added.

A snapshot recalls instantly (code::~rec.recall(\intro)::) or morphs over beats (code::~rec.morph(\intro, beats: 8)::): numeric sources ramp (link::Classes/REScorePlayer#*ramp::), the registered controls ramp through their setter along their spec's warp (link::Classes/REScorePlayer#*rampControl::), the rest switch at once; both are recorded as one strong::morph:: event pointing at the snapshot. A registered control is in a snapshot when it was given a getter: code::~rec.addControl(\knob_mix, spec, get: { ~mix }, set: { |v| ~mix = v; ~reverb.set(\mix, v) })::.

subsection:: Editing

Every operation answers a new score; code::edited(op):: applies one named by a dictionary (what a page sends): code::replacedSegment:: (an event of the control, a span, points), code::moved:: (an event, a beat), code::removed:: (ids, with what descends from them), code::changed:: (an event, a value), code::shifted::. The rig's lanes view edits that way (HomewareSC's code::view.html::: draw over a lane, drag, delete; undo, save in place or as a new file). Its live side is an link::Classes/REScoreEditor::: OSCdefs under code::<prefix>/rec/:: (play, stopPlay, edit, undo, save, revert) on a folder of takes, the take being edited kept as a working copy next to it (code::<take>.edit.json::, what the page shows and Play plays), the feed back to the page's server. The remote rig makes one with the prefix code::/rig::; a piece makes one in the IDE, code::~ed = REScoreEditor(~song, ~rec):: (the prefix code::/<song>::, the recorder's folder), and HomewareSC's code::scripts/take.sh view <piece>/Scores:: serves the page on that folder.

code::
s = REScore.read(path);
s.shifted(-0.4).write(path2);                       // the whole take 0.4 beat earlier (a measured latency)
s.quantized(0.25, voices: [\lead], strength: 0.5);
s.trimmed(8, 16).scaled(2);                          // the second bar, twice as slow
s.thinned(0.002).smoothed(5);                        // a knob curve without its jitter
s.replacedSegment([\midi, \knob_mix], 4, 8, [[4, 0.2], [6, 0.9], [8, 0.2]]);
::

Every operation answers a new score (the receiver stays, ids are kept, seconds follow the beats through the tempo map): code::shifted::, code::scaled::, code::quantized::, code::retimed:: (a function of the beat), code::trimmed:: (the latest snapshot before the start kept at 0), code::thinned::, code::smoothed::, code::withoutVoices::, code::onlyVoices::, code::withoutKinds::, code::renamedVoice::, code::replacedSegment:: (what a curve editor sends: new points of one control over a span), code::valueAt::. See link::Classes/REScore#Editing::.

subsection:: Visuals

code::
~viz = REScoreVisuals(~song.scoreRecorder);   // every take from now on, as it is made
~viz.attachPlayer(~p);                        // and a replay, with its playhead
~viz.detach;
::

link::Classes/REScoreVisuals:: sends what the recorder records and what a player plays to HomewareVisuals through link::Classes/RCVisuals:: (code::/score/take::, code::events::, code::event::, code::played::, code::head::, code::stop::, code::stopPlay::, one JSON string each; the protocol is the class file's header), and the score page there (code::public/score/::, its engine code::public/lib/score-viz.js::) draws the lanes under the head, following; code::scripts/viz-score-demo.sh:: there feeds it a performance from headless sclang. The remote rig attaches one to its recorder and to its players.

subsection:: A take rendered with its visuals

A section the live server cannot play whole (dense growths, a swarm) is recorded as a take all the same (the audio may break up: the take holds the lines and the inputs), or written as one from a sheet of code lines at beats (HomewareSC's code::take.sh cue::), then rendered offline with its visuals: code::scripts/take.sh render <take> --viz:: writes next to the take the WAV (the field undecoded), the visuals tape code::<take>.viz.json:: (link::Classes/REVisualsTape::: what the piece sent through RCVisuals, on the WAV's timeline) and a sidecar code::<take>.render.json:: (link::Classes/REScore#*readRender::). The levels and positions a rig's tap sends live through SendReply cannot come out of NRT: in a render the rigs' taps write their frames into buffers (link::Classes/REOfflineTap::), written at the end and merged into the tape by HomewareSC's Python side. link::Classes/RERenderedPlayer:: then plays the render live, next to the takes the program plays: the WAV summed into the chain and decoded as the instruments are, the tape sent through RCVisuals, both from the take's aligned start, no event fired but the tempo ones, every event reported; link::Classes/REScoreEditor#-play:: makes one when a take has a render (the lanes page's Play).

subsection:: Code

With code::\code:: among the inputs, the recorder wraps the interpreter's code::preProcessor:: (it returns the code unchanged, after opening a cause) and chains its code::codeDump:: (which closes it with the text); both are restored by code::disarm::. Lines matching code::exit::, code::recompile::, code::boot::, code::quit::, shell commands are stored but marked code::replay: false::; flip it per event when the line is harmless. A line that raises never reaches code::codeDump::: its cause is closed by the next line and the event is marked code::raised: true::; its effects are what it did before raising. A line that sends OSC (code::sendMsg::, code::sendBundle::) to this song's own port is marked code::replay: false:: with code::loopback:: naming the input its message came back as (within code::REScoreRecorder.loopbackWindow::, when the line names the path or a loopback address), and the input carries code::loopbackOf::: the message replays once, through its def. A line that sends elsewhere (Chataigne, the visuals) replays, since the other side needs it.

section:: The take file

A link::Classes/REScore:: is one JSON file, written by link::Classes/REJSON:: and read back exactly (sclang's code::parseJSON:: returns every scalar as a String: the reader types them again), readable by the Python tools and the pages. Paths follow link::Classes/RETake#*path::: code::<root>/<stamp>_<song> <version>.json::, tracked in git under the piece folder (code::<piece>/Scores/::) or the agent project (code::<try>/scores/::): scores are compositions, not audio.

code::
{
  "format": "re-score", "version": 1,
  "song": "demo", "piece": "HadronLake3", "wersion": "w1", "created": "261001_143012",
  "sc": "3.14.1", "commits": {"HomewareSC": "ec10dd29", "vikn": "b21e028"},
  "beat0": 128.0, "time0": 512.5, "tempo": 2.0, "latency": 0.2, "duration": 64.0,
  "tempoMap": [[0, 2.0]],
  "voices": {"core/kick": {"objects": [{"rc": "beat", "layer": "core", "name": "kick"}]}},
  "controls": {"knob_mix": {"min": 0, "max": 1, "warp": "lin", "default": 0.3, "unit": ""}},
  "events": [
    {"id": 1, "beat": 0.0, "secs": 0.0, "kind": "snapshot", "name": "start", "state": {...}},
    {"id": 2, "beat": 1.0, "secs": 0.5, "kind": "action", "voice": "core/kick",
     "rc": {"rc": "beat", "layer": "core", "name": "kick"}, "method": "set", "args": ["\\amp", 0.5, null, "\\default"]},
    {"id": 3, "beat": 2.0, "secs": 1.0, "kind": "midi", "voice": "knob_mix", "name": "knob_mix", "raw": 64, "value": 0.504},
    {"id": 4, "beat": 2.5, "secs": 1.25, "kind": "osc", "voice": "demo_scene_init", "key": "demo_scene_init",
     "path": "/demo/scene/init", "args": [1]},
    {"id": 5, "beat": 3.0, "secs": 1.5, "kind": "keyboard", "voice": "Seaboard RISE 2", "msg": "noteOn",
     "chan": 2, "note": 60, "value": 100},
    {"id": 6, "beat": 4.0, "secs": 2.0, "kind": "code", "voice": "code", "text": "~kick.set(\\amp, 0.2)", "replay": true},
    {"id": 7, "beat": 4.0, "secs": 2.0, "kind": "action", "cause": 6, "voice": "core/kick",
     "rc": {"rc": "beat", "layer": "core", "name": "kick"}, "method": "set", "args": ["\\amp", 0.2, null, "\\default"]},
    {"id": 8, "beat": 8.0, "secs": 4.0, "kind": "tempo", "tempo": 2.2}
  ]
}
::

table::
## code::beat::, code::secs:: || on the song's clock, from code::beat0:: / code::time0:: (an OSC message stamped by its sender: at the stamp); replay follows the clock, offline rendering uses seconds through the tempo map
## code::id:: || unique in the file, stable across edits; code::cause:: names the event that produced this one
## code::kind:: || code::action::, code::midi::, code::osc::, code::keyboard::, code::code::, code::snapshot::, code::morph::, code::tempo::
## code::voice:: || see link::#Voices::
## code::rc:: || an object reference: code::{"rc": "beat"|"layer"|"song"|"batch"|"orgnsm"|"fobject"|"crawler", "name": ..., "layer": ...}::, resolved at replay through the song (beats, layers, orgnsms, fobjects) or the current environment (batches, crawlers, by the variable that holds them)
## code::replay:: || code::false:: marks an event that must not be fired (an unsafe code line, an argument that could not be serialized, a line whose message came back as an input); absent means true
## code::raised:: || code::true:: on a code line that raised in the take: its effects are what it did before
## code::fallback:: || on a root event, what plays when it fails at replay: absent, the effects it did not reach again; code::false::, none; code::true::, all
## code::loopback::, code::loopbackOf:: || a line that sent a message to this song and the input it came back as, each naming the other
## code::quant:: (in the metadata) || the grid the take started on; a replay starts on the take's phase of it
## code::level:: || 1 (a human's act and its effects), 2 (the program's work: the companion file, see link::#Levels::)
## code::by:: || on a level 2 event, the object whose loop did it (a reference or a name)
## code::defer:: || code::true:: on a source: it is not fired at replay, its effects and program events are
## code::levels::, code::unrecorded::, code::check:: (in the metadata) || the levels written ([1, 2] when a companion exists), the program actions seen while level 2 was off, what threatens an exact replay
## code::inputs::, code::randData:: (in the metadata) || the kinds of input recorded; the main thread's random state at the start (three integers), given to the player's Routine
::

Values: numbers and strings as JSON; a Symbol as a string with a leading backslash (code::"\\amp"::, the pick-file convention); a Boolean and null as themselves; an Array as an array; an Event or Dictionary as an object. A Pattern, a Function with source, a Ref or any object with a meaningful compile string becomes code::{"sc": "Pseq([1, 2], inf)"}::, replayable in sclang and shown as text elsewhere; a string that would be read as something else (code::"42"::, code::"true"::, a leading backslash) is wrapped as code::{"str": "42"}::; a reCurrent object as its code::rc:: reference; anything else as code::{"ref": "<print string>"}::, which marks the event code::replay: false::. Floats are written with 17 significant digits; a non-finite number is written as null with a warning. Integers above 2^31 do not exist in sclang: ids count from 1 and times are floats.

section:: Status

Written in this order, each step on its own commits: the format, link::Classes/REJSON:: and link::Classes/REScore:: (read, write, the value codec); link::Classes/RETap::, link::Classes/REScoreRecorder:: and the taps; the code input; link::Classes/REScorePlayer:: (live replay, loops, overdub, morphs, curve and note patterns); the remote rig of HomewareSC (code::scripts/rig.sh::: a phone page that plays a sound design try, records takes, replays them and renders them offline); the edit operations; the lanes view of a take (the rig's code::view.html::, a playhead from the live side); the levels (link::#Levels::: the tagged Routines, the companion file, defer, the check); the offline clock (link::Classes/REOfflineClock:: and code::RECollectAddr::, probed: a song's beats and a take replayed on it, stepped, render through Score.recordNRT; HomewareSC's code::scripts/sc/offline_probe.scd::); a piece's take rendered offline as one command (HomewareSC's code::scripts/take.sh render::: the piece's files, named by its code::PIECE_FILES.txt::, loaded on the offline clock against a collecting server that counts as running, the take replayed from its phase of the grid, the library's SynthDefs and the collected buffer and node messages rendered through Score.recordNRT; code::RECollectAddr:: answers a sync at once); level 3 (link::#Levels::: link::Classes/RETapAddr::, the server's messages in code::<take>.l3.json:: with the defs next to the take, rendered as they were by code::take.sh render --level 3::, checked against the program's render); editing on the page (the rig's lanes view sends code::REScore.edited:: operations to the live side, which keeps a working copy next to the take, undoes, saves in place or as a new file); the visuals feed (link::#Visuals::: link::Classes/REScoreVisuals:: and HomewareVisuals' score page); the editing page for a piece (link::Classes/REScoreEditor::, the transport and the editing of the rig's live side as a class on any song and folder; HomewareSC's code::take.sh view:: serves the lanes view on a piece's Scores folder; the lanes scroll, the score page zooms and scrolls its lanes); a take rendered with its visuals (link::#A take rendered with its visuals::: link::Classes/REVisualsTape::, link::Classes/REOfflineTap::, link::Classes/RERenderedPlayer::, the editor's Play on a render; HomewareSC's code::take.sh render --viz:: and code::take.sh cue::).
